00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.791
- Gary, why don't we start
off by, why don't you tell

00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:00.977
us a little bit about what VaynerMedia is?

00:01:00.977 --> 00:01:02.543
- You know, obviously
you saw in the video,

00:01:02.543 --> 00:01:04.940
I try to make it as simple as possible.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:09.940
All we really are is a agency.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.950
I think what makes us different is,

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.080
because I didn't come
from the agency landscape

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.660
and more importantly,
because I built VaynerMedia

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.890
with the intent to be a platform

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.484
for buying businesses and brands

00:01:20.484 --> 00:01:23.150
during the next economic downturn,

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:24.500
it's been built very differently.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.090
The first thing that was different

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:28.780
was that we have media and
creative under one house.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.410
Not part of a holding company
where it's separate companies

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.190
but from day one, it was
built where both media

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.810
and creative sit together and the context

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.280
and the end consumer is
truly, not through reporting,

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.060
not through data but
truly at the forefront

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.870
and we think about math
and art as friction

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.560
to create diamonds, not one is
more valuable than the other.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.960
And then really, the only
other big variable difference

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.766
is it's a dictatorship, you know?

00:01:54.766 --> 00:01:56.050
(audience laughs)

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.970
and what I mean by that
is, I don't have a board,

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.920
I don't have a holding
co, everything wrong

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.630
at Vayner is 100% my fault
and when something's run

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.690
by an entrepreneurial operator

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.140
a lot of good can happen in growth

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.880
and a lot of growing pains
and silly young things happen

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.650
and I definitely think we're
dramatically more contemporary

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.920
than every alternative
at the global scale.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.520
We have Singapore, London,
we have a thousand people.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.110
It's not a small company.

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.270
I spent a lot of time
actually, strategically

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:33.910
making it about me, look over
here, look at this crazy guy,

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.500
so that I can operate
something that I think

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.900
is quite disruptive over
here and I'm proud of that.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.530
But I think it's a very different shop

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.440
because it comes from a
very different agenda.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.970
We're not held accountable
to every 90 day finance goals

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.020
and I think that has
been a massive advantage.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:56.690
- Yeah, no quarterly earnings, huh?

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.101
- No and no getting on
a call with analysts

00:03:00.101 --> 00:03:03.550
and appeasing some short-term narrative

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.333
that's an inside vigged game.

00:03:06.391 --> 00:03:09.224
(audience laughs)

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.340
- I remember a meeting
that we had at one point

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.950
where you came to us and said,

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.310
you would bring new
channels to us, for example

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:22.700
and with Pinterest and you
talked to us about Pinterest

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.027
and one time you came and you said,

00:03:24.027 --> 00:03:25.967
"You guys gotta figure out Snapchat.

00:03:25.967 --> 00:03:28.640
"You gotta be on
Snapchat, it's the future"

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.197
and at that point, it was just something

00:03:30.197 --> 00:03:34.149
that my kids did and I
couldn't figure it out.

00:03:34.149 --> 00:03:37.857
What's next and what's new in that space

00:03:37.857 --> 00:03:38.690
that we should be looking at?

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.464
- It's interesting Trish, even in that,

00:03:40.464 --> 00:03:44.090
it's very rare, very
rare though I'm a human,

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.020
I'm sure I slip up but I would argue

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.530
if we had film of me
bringing up Snap in time

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.730
that it was more likely
that I didn't refer to it

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.470
as the future and I never predict.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.920
I'm just completely
obsessed with right now.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.740
What I know right this
second, as we sit here

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.300
and everybody watching around the world,

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.860
that LinkedIn and TikTok
are the two platforms

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.450
that give you organic reach.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.950
If you do not pay media,
if you're a nobody

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:12.500
and you post on either one
of those two platforms,

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:16.079
something great can
happen that cannot happen

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:20.060
outside of a miracle 0.00001% chance

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.410
on Facebook, Twitter,
Instagram and YouTube.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.400
Those things always fascinate me

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.683
because I love under-priced real estate.

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:30.090
If I came to somebody 25 years ago

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:31.613
and said there's an island
called Turks and Caicos

00:04:31.613 --> 00:04:34.730
that I genuinely believed
more people will go to it

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.889
over the next 25 years
and we bought up property,

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:38.720
everybody would be happy.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.040
So I spend all my time on two core things.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.470
One, under-priced organic reach,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:48.470
which often is more at a small,
entrepreneurial SMB level.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:50.900
At the corporate level they're important

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.989
because I want companies to get the reps

00:04:52.989 --> 00:04:55.010
of understanding the nuances

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.030
that are making this platform pop

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.040
and understanding LinkedIn
today is very different

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.670
than all of us understanding
it four years ago.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.080
It's a completely different beast.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.670
LinkedIn today acts much more

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.210
like Facebook did six years ago

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.810
than the LinkedIn that
we all grew up using,

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:12.263
which was a tool, not a content platform.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.661
The things I'm learning on TikTok now

00:05:14.661 --> 00:05:17.010
will matter because those
are getting instilled

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.333
into 12 and 19 year olds right now

00:05:19.333 --> 00:05:21.830
and those will become
nuances that quite matter

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.860
in communication in three,
five, seven, nine years.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.340
Obviously, Chase is less
likely to be obsessed

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:31.403
with understanding TikTok,
in comparison to Vans

00:05:31.403 --> 00:05:33.850
or in comparison to Mattel, right?

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:36.210
So you think about all those things

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.120
but I don't know what's next.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.930
What I do know is that I'm incredibly good

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:44.560
at understanding, as a human,
subjectively, intuitively,

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.400
of what seems to be current

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.670
and then I put a ton of
discipline and work ethic

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:53.170
and dollars and effort into
proving those hypotheses

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.133
to be true or false.

00:05:56.631 --> 00:06:00.853
- So shifting a little,
if you think about,

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:03.480
since it's in the news

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:04.313
- Okay.

00:06:04.313 --> 00:06:06.890
- Interested in your thoughts on Twitter's

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:09.620
recent announcement about
banning political ads,

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:12.113
without getting political
in our discussion but--

00:06:12.113 --> 00:06:15.100
- I think the platforms
are making a ridiculously,

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.580
personally, I think it's smart.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.468
I think that platform's
revenue from political ads

00:06:20.468 --> 00:06:22.880
is not significant enough in return

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.680
for what the headlining
and positioning means to it

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.170
and all the vulnerabilities of it.

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:31.870
We are in a fully on-tilt,
headline reading society.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:33.330
The amount of opinions in this room,

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.530
from the two of us to
everybody in this room,

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:36.920
to everybody who's watching,

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.480
where we have deep
convictions and thoughts,

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:43.543
predicated on one or two
headlines is laughable.

00:06:44.524 --> 00:06:47.210
So A, I think it was a smart move by Jack.

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:50.331
I think it helps Twitter but I think they

00:06:50.331 --> 00:06:53.880
the platforms have done
an incredibly bad job

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.600
in distinguishing what a platform is.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.760
People are way on tilt on believing

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.560
that they're manipulating something.

00:07:03.029 --> 00:07:06.259
Mark's opportunity when AOC asks him,

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.360
will you police this truth?

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.800
Is easily rebuttaled with,

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.013
can we have CNN and Fox do that too?

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.031
We are at a very funny place

00:07:15.031 --> 00:07:18.800
where ask modern technologies to do things

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.113
that we don't hold traditional
technologies accountable for.

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:28.710
So great but I think in the
macro, they're doing a poor job.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:32.320
I'm very curious what
the variable difference

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.280
is between not fact checking an ad

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.280
in a Facebook feed,
versus not checking a fact

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.053
coming out of a news anchor's
mouth on a news platform.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:46.650
- Well let me push back just a little bit.

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:49.103
- Please, I love pushing back a bit.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.422
- So we do a lot of work right now

00:07:54.422 --> 00:07:56.913
looking at the impact of bots.

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:01.120
On escalating news on social media

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.310
in a way that if you just saw an article

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:06.920
in the New York Times that it might not

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:08.670
move at the same pace.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:13.280
And how do you see bots
impacting the spread of news

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.350
and the spread of fake news, for example?

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.540
- I don't view technology any different

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:19.870
than I look at humans.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:21.890
This is one big game of communication.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:26.010
Do we have data that
shows what Carson Tucker

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:29.680
or Don Lemon mean when they say something?

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.150
That's my pushback, my pushback

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:34.350
is when are we gonna hold ourselves

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:36.023
accountable for the information?

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:43.810
Whether a bot is run to run
more ads or more potential

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.740
awareness against a headline,

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:52.200
or a newscaster uses an adjective in a way

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:55.570
that pushes that information, I genuinely

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:58.220
do not see the variable
difference to the end consumer.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:01.020
I would argue people are
hearing what they want to hear.

00:09:02.731 --> 00:09:05.415
I don't know, I see plenty
of things coming on Facebook

00:09:05.415 --> 00:09:09.990
and CNN and Fox and Twitter
that I'm a big enough boy

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:14.240
to decipher what I want to
believe or tend to believe

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.280
or I'm curious about.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.040
This notion that we're so helpless

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:19.860
that the second that we see something,

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.890
we believe it to be true,
I think it's the reverse.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.940
I think we're justifying
what we want to be true

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:27.880
through the propaganda.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.650
- So how should we then consume news

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.853
as an educated consumer and be discerning.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:35.623
- By putting in the work.

00:09:37.327 --> 00:09:39.200
You know how many people here
are gonna be keyboard warriors

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.450
about politics and not
even register to vote?

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:46.640
How should we do it?

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.290
The same way we do everything.

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:50.020
Remember when you blindly
believed everything

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:51.770
out of your parents mouth?

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:54.403
You grew up, it's time
we do that with media.

00:09:55.479 --> 00:09:58.479
(audience applauds)

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:03.390
- So last year you published
the book "Crushing it"

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:07.917
which is a bit of an evolution
from the 2009 "Crush it"

00:10:09.046 --> 00:10:10.235
- Thank you.

00:10:10.235 --> 00:10:13.162
(audience laughs)

00:10:13.162 --> 00:10:14.680
- And so, tell us about what has evolved

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.606
from a trend perspective of branding.

00:10:17.606 --> 00:10:19.250
What's new and different now,

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:21.200
what's stayed the same and what has died?

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.136
- When I wrote "Crush it" in 2008

00:10:23.136 --> 00:10:24.660
and then the book came out in '09,

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:28.740
the thought of building a
viable business or lifestyle

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.870
on the back of YouTube or
Twitter was a laughable event.

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.826
If you go to amazon right
now and look at the first

00:10:33.826 --> 00:10:38.340
500 reviews of "Crush it"
that were published in 2009,

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:39.657
basically it's a mix of

00:10:39.657 --> 00:10:42.520
"this guy's completely
"a snake oil salesman"

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.420
to "social media will be gone next year".

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.160
There was not a belief that
enough scale could happen

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.760
on these platforms to
create something meaningful.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:54.251
So what's evolved is the
acceptance from the masses

00:10:54.251 --> 00:10:57.250
that there is power
behind these platforms.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:00.700
We've gone from 2009, where
people didn't believe me

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:02.450
that these platforms were powerful enough

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:04.890
for you to be able to make $80,000 a year

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:07.860
to us now feeling like we have
to break up these companies

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:11.320
'cause they're so powerful
in dictating our minds.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.800
So that's changed, the
perception of the power

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.977
of social networks, which oh by the way,

00:11:15.977 --> 00:11:17.700
are just the current state

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:20.580
of where our tension is
on the internet itself.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:23.480
Whether this goes to AR or VR, or voice

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:26.453
all this is is where are
we paying attention to?

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:30.164
So that's the biggest change.

00:11:30.164 --> 00:11:33.050
The things that are the same are my belief

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:37.330
that there's a lot of people
here in this room watching

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:41.380
who have a certain perspective

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:43.410
on how they're gonna live their life

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:46.310
and what is gonna bring
the most happiness to them

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:48.220
and I wanna continue to beat a drum

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.370
of do not underestimate
the power of this platform.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:53.155
Do not underestimate what it feels like

00:11:53.155 --> 00:11:58.155
to be desperately happy
making $103,000 a year

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:01.840
doing something you love,
versus being unhappy

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:05.560
making $197,000 a year
spending it on things

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:07.233
to disguise your unhappiness.

00:12:10.338 --> 00:12:12.680
- And you do talk a lot
on your social channels

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:17.680
about hating your job and while obviously,

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:19.825
everyone here loves their job.

00:12:19.825 --> 00:12:21.420
(audience laughs)

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:22.960
I mean we all go through points,

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:25.820
whether you're on a
non-traditional career path

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:27.950
like some of the influencers
that you work with

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:29.800
that are entrepreneurs or even a more

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:32.190
traditional path like the ones here.

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:35.170
What advice, what could we learn,

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:37.490
for those of us on a more traditional path

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:40.500
from the non-traditional
folks that you work with?

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.970
- You know it's funny, it's really hard

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:47.270
to ask a giraffe to be
a penguin or vice versa

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:50.080
and so one thing that's
become much more obvious to me

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:51.940
as I've gotten older is like wow,

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:55.900
DNA is powerful, I'm so
comfortable with "no"

00:12:55.900 --> 00:13:00.370
and losing and fear but I'm so empathetic

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:01.620
to people that aren't.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:05.180
I and this will surprise a lot
of you that maybe follow me,

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:08.620
I'm unbelievably comfortable
with aggressiveness

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:12.470
and candor and binary talk on stage

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:15.110
but one-on-one, I've really struggled

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:17.160
over the last 25 years as an executive

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.150
with radical candor and
shooting it straight

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:23.060
because I'm so optimistic and
I'm always trying to fix it

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:24.900
and so that's taken me a lot of work

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:27.917
and through that work, I'm like okay,

00:13:27.917 --> 00:13:31.120
there's the things that come
natural and things that aren't.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.626
If you grew up in a framework with parents

00:13:33.626 --> 00:13:38.626
or customs that really,
desperately over valued

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:43.762
outside affirmation, the
opinions of your parents'

00:13:43.762 --> 00:13:47.100
parents or siblings,
because you come from,

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:49.730
let's call it what it is,
an immigrant background

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:51.680
where there's a huge commonality there.

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:55.600
School, you were so
deeply bought into school

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:58.460
that the short term
affirmation of every 90 days

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:01.610
with grades and report
cards made you comfortable.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.471
It's very hard to then go into hey,

00:14:04.471 --> 00:14:07.398
this is what you should
learn from entrepreneur land.

00:14:07.398 --> 00:14:09.970
Don't worry about it,
it's all gonna be fine.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.500
That's very hard, you've
fully built yourself

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:16.600
as an animal to completely
value outside judgment

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:18.670
that is not based on the market but based

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:19.820
on another human being.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:23.160
That's what I'd like people to learn,

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.230
which is like hey, what are we doing here?

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:28.390
You've won the four hundred
trillion to one lottery

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:30.350
of being a human being, do you really

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:31.990
wanna live with regret?

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:33.400
Do you not understand that the internet

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:36.070
is the greatest optionality
in the history of mankind?

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:38.050
That our grandparents
and everybody behind them

00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:40.900
had nowhere close to the
options that you sit with today?

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:42.949
And really, what are you valuing

00:14:42.949 --> 00:14:45.410
that's making you conform
into doing something

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:48.340
that doesn't make you happy,
your current overhead?

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:50.230
So sell your home and rent.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:52.440
That sounds crazy, you know when I started

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:55.560
talking about hey, in
the pursuit of happiness,

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:59.710
would you consider selling
your home and renting?

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:03.040
I got destroyed in the comments sections

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.790
and a lot of the places like
that I'm some horrible person.

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:09.418
Like, how is living your
life to pay the bills

00:15:09.418 --> 00:15:12.000
that you've created for yourself,

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.840
all that humans do are
create their own jails

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:16.720
and then live within it.

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:20.804
That's all we do, so knowing
that that's very hard

00:15:20.804 --> 00:15:23.800
in a human way, the situation

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:25.060
that you have with your parents,

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:27.470
all that stuff, the professional one

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:30.500
feels like I have a prayer
of communicating that

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:34.190
and here's my point, if you
wanna be a professional skier

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:38.450
or you wanna start a blog
or podcast around cooking,

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:43.060
my big thing is, if you
jump and start swimming,

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:48.060
this is riding a bike, kissing
a boy or girl or swimming.

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:50.460
It seems super scary until you do it

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:53.710
and then you just laugh
about why it felt so scary.

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:56.430
If you go do that thing
and live more humble,

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:59.533
live with judgment, you
could always get a job again.

00:16:01.217 --> 00:16:05.620
I don't know, my great fear is regret

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:07.660
because it is the obvious
thing I see in people

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:11.430
that are 80 to 100 years
old that seems super scary.

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:14.313
Nobody was thrilled that
they played it safe.

00:16:15.673 --> 00:16:17.510
It's just, you don't see
it and I think people

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:19.140
should spend more time with
the 80 to 100 year olds

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:20.713
that aren't their grandparents.

00:16:20.713 --> 00:16:21.546
(audience laughs)

00:16:21.546 --> 00:16:23.343
I mean it, I mean it, people need context

00:16:23.343 --> 00:16:24.840
on life a little bit.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:27.250
I think people have a horrible
relationship with time.

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:28.380
Do you know how many people in this room

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:33.380
are scared shitless of 30, like
it's some, it means nothing.

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:37.290
But our society's decided to tell you

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:39.100
that you have to figure it out and marry

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:42.030
and children and what
are you talking about?

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:45.990
98% of the 59 year olds I know
don't have it figured out.

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:48.660
The hell are we supposed to
have it all figured at 30?

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:52.320
25, like people are
making terrible decisions,

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.610
getting married because they think

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:55.323
they're supposed to by this age.

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.290
Buying homes because they
think they're supposed to.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:04.290
The rules of modern society have lead to,

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:09.010
everybody wants to blame technology

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:13.800
and drug companies for all our problems.

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:18.540
We need to blame our
norms, our expectations

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:22.803
our ridiculous north
stars that make no sense.

00:17:25.117 --> 00:17:26.400
When I look at this young of a crowd,

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:28.330
they're living to 110.

00:17:28.330 --> 00:17:30.440
There's an enormous
amount of people in here

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:33.414
that are gonna live to
110 and are freaking out

00:17:33.414 --> 00:17:36.410
that they don't have it figured out by 30.

00:17:36.410 --> 00:17:38.706
You're not even a fucking
quarter of the way there yet.

00:17:38.706 --> 00:17:39.850
(audience laughs)

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.340
what are we talking about?

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:45.730
So, that's very heady stuff
in its practical layer

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:50.500
of business and life and jobs and career,

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:53.130
I think everybody owes it to themselves

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:55.310
to start something on the side

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:57.683
around something that
they're really, desperately.

00:17:57.683 --> 00:18:02.255
The reason a lot of entrepreneurs
are alco, workaholics

00:18:02.255 --> 00:18:04.153
(audience laughs)

