If they happen to have that DNA

00:25:40.822 --> 00:25:43.050
and that makes them, the only thing

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:47.360
I want my kids to do is be as
happy as I am in what they do.

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:49.830
If they look at my and look,
now there's a new variable.

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:52.460
Things with me continue to
grow, I'm very empathetic

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:53.337
but they may look at it and be like,

00:25:53.337 --> 00:25:54.980
"I don't want any part of that".

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:57.840
And I'm gonna go to Peru and
give away all his money, right?

00:25:57.840 --> 00:25:59.306
And I'm good with that.

00:25:59.306 --> 00:26:00.139
(audience laughs)

00:26:00.139 --> 00:26:01.601
I really am, I'm really good with that.

00:26:01.601 --> 00:26:05.620
I really, really just want
them to find their thing

00:26:05.620 --> 00:26:07.930
and if their thing is something

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:10.610
that I am maybe not as passionate about,

00:26:10.610 --> 00:26:12.711
which is be more within the system,

00:26:12.711 --> 00:26:15.827
create less risk, my kids could say,

00:26:15.827 --> 00:26:17.077
"You know what, I hate entrepreneurship,

00:26:17.077 --> 00:26:19.570
"it made Dad travel a lot, I hate it."

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:23.413
that makes sense to me
and so my communication

00:26:23.413 --> 00:26:25.830
with my children is more about them

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:29.030
truly looking for their happiness

00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:31.130
and understanding the world will evolve

00:26:31.130 --> 00:26:33.620
quite a bit by the time
they hit the real world

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:38.330
and leaning into the
happiness but practically.

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:41.370
You wanna go paint with tomatoes in Peru?

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:45.253
That's amazing, just FYI, I'm
not giving you one dollar.

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:51.144
And that's amazing, like
to me if they're gonna live

00:26:51.144 --> 00:26:53.200
with four friends in a commune,

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:56.170
that's tremendous, that's awesome.

00:26:56.170 --> 00:27:00.100
To me, it's just about
merit and happiness.

00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:01.980
Practical optimism.

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:04.130
- So this is a early in career

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:06.130
audience that you've got here.

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:09.300
What is some practical actual advice

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:12.153
that they could take away and
how are they gonna advance

00:27:12.153 --> 00:27:14.040
themselves, advance their careers

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:16.370
from a personal branding perspective?

00:27:16.370 --> 00:27:19.670
Something they'd walk out of
the room and do immediately.

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:22.750
- So my number, number
one thing that I think

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:26.890
may be very unique and
couldn't be more passionate

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:29.550
in delivering to this
audience is the following.

00:27:29.550 --> 00:27:32.740
I really highly recommend that
you die on your own sword.

00:27:32.740 --> 00:27:34.740
Let me explain what I mean by that.

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:38.290
Every day, a lot of you are in meetings

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:39.520
where things are being talked about

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:41.800
that you may have some insights, opinions

00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:45.400
and actual knowledge about and you are not

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:48.040
communicating an opposing view

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:50.080
because you know that it won't be accepted

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:52.000
by the managers above you

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:55.640
and you think it's a
vulnerability by communicating it.

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:57.460
And I think it's the reverse.

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:59.920
I think there's an incredibly soft

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:04.140
and respectful way for
you to create a data point

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:06.870
to the conversation and that I believe

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:08.690
this is what I see a lot happening,

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:11.060
where people don't say anything,

00:28:11.060 --> 00:28:12.820
'cause they're appeasing how to move up,

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:14.710
manager to manager to manager.

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:19.710
The world actually changes
and then the company resets

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:23.330
and the executive, by
not saying something,

00:28:23.330 --> 00:28:26.000
gets viewed upon as, that
was their point of view

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:30.360
and they get hurt by
pandering to the machine,

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.640
versus by being historically
correct in the room.

00:28:33.640 --> 00:28:35.740
When all those executives either grow

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:39.230
within the organization or
disperse to other organizations,

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:41.810
they become an interesting
prospect to bring along,

00:28:41.810 --> 00:28:43.690
because they were historically correct.

00:28:43.690 --> 00:28:46.163
I'm giving advice that I
take as an entrepreneur.

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:49.310
My businesses are always much slower

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:51.900
and smaller upfront because I'm often

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:53.760
talking about and executing on something

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:56.540
that hasn't been accepted
in the macro yet.

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:58.493
And all the upside is on the back end.

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:01.210
I think people in here are sitting

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:06.210
on their words and they
shouldn't and everybody knows it.

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:11.030
This entire room is in meetings every day

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:14.360
where we talk about things
that they don't agree with

00:29:14.360 --> 00:29:16.010
and they don't say a single word.

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:21.152
I don't think you should
say 'em the way I do

00:29:21.152 --> 00:29:22.470
(audience laughs)

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:24.640
I say them aggressively in my Jersey style

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:26.914
because I have nobody who can fire me.

00:29:26.914 --> 00:29:28.610
(audience laughs)

00:29:28.610 --> 00:29:30.350
I mean that, the client can fire me

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:33.010
and that would be my
fault but I understand

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:36.377
why executives can't do what I do.

00:29:36.377 --> 00:29:38.100
But if I was a senior executive

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:40.490
or my first day intern, knowing

00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:42.100
my communication style of DNA,

00:29:42.100 --> 00:29:44.630
I would just do it very politely and soft

00:29:44.630 --> 00:29:46.960
but I would say look, I
don't think banner ads,

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:50.170
even though the data shows
that it's doing well,

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:53.140
I think we're only looking
at short term conversions

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:56.550
and not factoring in LTV
and it has decline in CAC

00:29:56.550 --> 00:29:58.093
and what's the value of that customer.

00:29:58.093 --> 00:30:00.330
I would share my thoughts.

00:30:00.330 --> 00:30:02.390
The key that I notice is,

00:30:02.390 --> 00:30:04.510
but you have to know what
you're talking about.

00:30:04.510 --> 00:30:05.720
Where a lot of people get caught

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:07.970
is they think they know
what they're talking about

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:10.100
and then they say something
and they're wrong.

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:11.600
One of the things that's
super interesting to me

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:14.163
is I talk about a very narrow thing.

00:30:15.260 --> 00:30:19.310
I stay very narrow, I'm just
not interested in being wrong.

00:30:19.310 --> 00:30:21.648
So I actually stay quiet
for all the talking I do,

00:30:21.648 --> 00:30:25.640
I'm quiet around 99.9% of things.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:26.473
It's why I don't get involved

00:30:26.473 --> 00:30:28.965
in a lot of other subject matters.

00:30:28.965 --> 00:30:31.370
I could talk about marketing, I could talk

00:30:31.370 --> 00:30:33.008
about the Jet's offense line woes

00:30:33.008 --> 00:30:34.410
(audience laughs)

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:36.990
I could talk about wine
but I keep it narrow

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:41.173
'cause I don't know about healthcare.

00:30:43.720 --> 00:30:45.061
- But you probably have an opinion.

00:30:45.061 --> 00:30:47.810
- That's for sure, every one of us does

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:48.740
but that's not the kind of thing

00:30:48.740 --> 00:30:52.393
that I would stake in the
ground for career advancement.

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:56.880
- So you do know a lot about wine,

00:30:56.880 --> 00:30:58.737
so tell us, what's your
favorite Sauvignon Blanc

00:30:58.737 --> 00:31:00.033
and your favorite cab?

00:31:01.147 --> 00:31:03.440
- My favorite Sauvignon Blancs tend to be

00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:06.163
from the Sancerre region in France,

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:11.620
because I tend to like
stoney, flinty flavors

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:14.640
more than the grass that
you get from New Zealand

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:17.765
and I think California
Sauvignon Blanc sucks.

00:31:17.765 --> 00:31:19.400
(audience laughs)

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:23.000
So, Sancerre, Lucien
Crochet is a great producer,

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.123
so I would say that and then on cab,

00:31:26.037 --> 00:31:29.850
I think cab is the most
overpriced wine in the world.

00:31:29.850 --> 00:31:32.705
It became very popular,
I think Napa Valley cabs

00:31:32.705 --> 00:31:36.958
are wildly overpriced, so for Cabernet,

00:31:36.958 --> 00:31:41.654
maybe a Bordeaux based cab from the Medoc

00:31:41.654 --> 00:31:43.930
because I tend to like a little more

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:45.683
vegetal and nuanced flavors.

00:31:47.210 --> 00:31:49.690
And by the way, it's similar to media.

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:51.710
It's not that I hate
television commercials,

00:31:51.710 --> 00:31:53.760
I just think there's better alternatives.

00:31:54.953 --> 00:31:57.411
If you love Cabernet and
I'm sure 90% of the people

00:31:57.411 --> 00:32:01.050
that like red wine like
to some degree to love it,

00:32:01.050 --> 00:32:04.410
I just know that there's
alternatives in Portugal,

00:32:04.410 --> 00:32:07.080
alternatives in Argentina that can appease

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:10.230
your big fruit, silky,
creamy, oaky flavors

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:11.830
for a third of the price.

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:15.060
It's never about anything
other than arbitrage for me.

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:17.830
So it's not that Napa cabs
suck or Cabernet sucks,

00:32:17.830 --> 00:32:19.980
it's that you're paying for the brand

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:22.720
of Napa cab and you're
not educated to know

00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:26.283
that you can get that fulfillment
from the Priorat in Spain.

00:32:29.716 --> 00:32:31.610
(audience laughs)

00:32:31.610 --> 00:32:32.781
- Okay, one more question--

00:32:32.781 --> 00:32:33.614
- And by the way, on that note,

00:32:33.614 --> 00:32:35.070
just to make a segue to some
of the stuff I talk about,

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.170
that is absolutely my thesis in marketing.

00:32:38.210 --> 00:32:40.060
I don't hate direct mail, you can show me

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:41.160
direct mail that works, I just don't know

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:43.940
that you've perfectly
executed one mile radius

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:45.820
Facebook ads with contextual creative

00:32:45.820 --> 00:32:47.363
to the person that's seeing it.

00:32:48.500 --> 00:32:49.333
That's all.

00:32:49.333 --> 00:32:52.500
- How did your wine business
lead to VaynerMedia.

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:56.150
- That, when I had the
awakening at 34 of like,

00:32:56.150 --> 00:32:58.860
wait a minute, I'm not a great retailer,

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:01.220
I'm a great communicator,
I built this business

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:03.520
from three to 60 million
dollars with no money

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:05.740
because I was right about
eCommerce, I was right

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:07.570
about email, I was right
about Google AdWords.

00:33:07.570 --> 00:33:09.900
I was right about YouTube,
I was right about Twitter.

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:13.240
All five things I just told
you, you guys are youngsters.

00:33:13.240 --> 00:33:15.410
When I started winelibrary.com in 1996,

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:18.800
people told me the internet
was a fad, the whole thing.

00:33:18.800 --> 00:33:22.353
Forget about Snapchat or Vine
or TikTok, the whole thing.

00:33:24.210 --> 00:33:26.100
People that sold the Yellow Pages

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:28.220
in a chamber of commerce
event made fun of me,

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:32.230
like, this kid's, he's gonna be wrong.

00:33:32.230 --> 00:33:33.700
Like he doesn't know, he's a child.

00:33:33.700 --> 00:33:36.380
And I was like, "The
consumer's always right".

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:38.340
I don't even feel like I'm a human being.

00:33:38.340 --> 00:33:40.210
All I think I am is a pass-through

00:33:40.210 --> 00:33:41.973
to what humans are actually doing.

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:46.210
I'm not right about TikTok,
humans are doing it.

00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:48.810
There's hundreds of
millions of people using it.

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:51.680
This is not prediction
time, this happened.

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:53.020
I love people like, what's next,

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:54.370
you're Nostradamus, you predict.

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:57.258
I'm like, I'm a post-game newscaster.

00:33:57.258 --> 00:33:58.420
(audience laughs)

00:33:58.420 --> 00:34:02.820
I'm not Nostradamus,
I'm like Howard Cosell.

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:05.860
This happened, podcasting happened.

00:34:05.860 --> 00:34:06.693
- He was a newscaster.

00:34:06.693 --> 00:34:08.270
(audience laughs)

00:34:08.270 --> 00:34:10.300
- LinkedIn happened, I've never

00:34:10.300 --> 00:34:11.940
predicted a thing in my life,

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:14.800
I'm just not scared to execute
on what's actually happening,

00:34:14.800 --> 00:34:16.668
'cause I'm not in the
business of making reports

00:34:16.668 --> 00:34:18.943
or politics my north star.

00:34:20.740 --> 00:34:22.690
- Why don't we open it up to the crowd?

00:34:23.860 --> 00:34:24.693
- Let's go.

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:27.610
- You probably need a mic

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:30.307
so that the people watching
the webcast can hear you.

00:34:31.406 --> 00:34:33.380
- And there's one over there for next.

00:34:33.380 --> 00:34:34.213
- How are you?

00:34:34.213 --> 00:34:36.070
- [Audience Member] Hey
Gary I'm doing well.

00:34:36.070 --> 00:34:38.717
My name's Dave Davis, how you doin' man.

00:34:38.717 --> 00:34:42.180
I have a question, so I'm
a father, I'm 29 years old.

00:34:42.180 --> 00:34:44.370
I have a four year old and my question

00:34:44.370 --> 00:34:47.749
pertains to, I want her to be safe,

00:34:47.749 --> 00:34:50.890
I wanna try to some degree help make sure

00:34:50.890 --> 00:34:52.280
she doesn't really get off track

00:34:52.280 --> 00:34:55.510
and provide her best school,
best things like that.

00:34:55.510 --> 00:34:58.160
But I know that you
mentioned your philosophy

00:34:58.160 --> 00:35:01.209
on your children and just
wanna get your advice

00:35:01.209 --> 00:35:03.463
from a parent perspective, right?

00:35:04.750 --> 00:35:06.167
When do you kinda say--

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:10.270
- I got it, look, first of all.

00:35:10.270 --> 00:35:13.070
My interest in giving any specific person

00:35:13.070 --> 00:35:18.070
parenting advice, is 0.0, I
think everybody needs to parent

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:20.000
the way they decide to parent.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:22.723
I like communicating the alternatives

00:35:22.723 --> 00:35:25.410
to what I think is being accepted,

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:28.100
especially no different
than TikTok happened.

00:35:28.100 --> 00:35:32.620
I, Gary Vaynerchuk, get
over a thousand DMs a week

00:35:32.620 --> 00:35:35.560
on Instagram from kids
that are deeply resentful

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:39.570
of their parents for
over-manipulating their lives.

00:35:39.570 --> 00:35:41.632
I'm talking about, I hate my parents

00:35:41.632 --> 00:35:44.323
'cause they pay for my Equinox and Uber.

00:35:44.323 --> 00:35:45.340
(audience laughs)

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:49.318
I reply and say, "Then stop
taking the money, dick".

00:35:49.318 --> 00:35:51.440
(audience laughs)

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:54.070
But I also know what's
going on at a higher level.

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:56.080
Which is parents are using their children

00:35:56.080 --> 00:35:59.580
as products to impress
their contemporaries.

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:01.140
The reason parents pay is not necessarily

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:02.790
because they wanna help
the kid, it's they want

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:05.740
their friend, their sister, their parents

00:36:05.740 --> 00:36:10.020
or others to think little
Johnny and Sally are successful.

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:13.363
So you need to do you but let me say this.

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:16.690
I don't understand why people

00:36:16.690 --> 00:36:18.493
can't remember them being kids.

00:36:20.291 --> 00:36:22.938
I love parents that are
like, "I restrict my kids

00:36:22.938 --> 00:36:26.307
"time on their phone", I'm
like, "They've seen porn".

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:32.330
Like this notion that we can
control life is laughable

00:36:32.390 --> 00:36:35.210
and more importantly, we're
giving indicators to kids

00:36:35.210 --> 00:36:38.760
that we don't trust them,
which creates self doubt

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:41.740
which really leads to
a lot of unhappiness.

00:36:41.740 --> 00:36:43.690
So look, you get to pick your north star.

00:36:43.690 --> 00:36:45.820
My north star is self-esteem

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:48.110
and accountability and happiness.

00:36:48.110 --> 00:36:53.110
Not Harvard, not entrepreneurship,
not winning, that.

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:55.700
I want them to be happy, big time.

00:36:55.700 --> 00:36:57.413
I want them to be accountable.

00:36:58.690 --> 00:37:01.390
I'll never forget, I was a
really good baseball player

00:37:01.390 --> 00:37:04.080
in third and fourth grade,
before talent kicked in

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:08.070
and this one kid, John
Longo was a real pitcher,

00:37:08.070 --> 00:37:10.475
big fat Italian kid,
if you're watching John

00:37:10.475 --> 00:37:12.850
(audience laughs)

00:37:12.850 --> 00:37:14.299
and because he was so
big, he could throw gas

00:37:14.299 --> 00:37:16.349
and he just overpowered me and I went 0-4

00:37:17.500 --> 00:37:19.163
and struck out four
times, which is unheard of

00:37:19.163 --> 00:37:22.050
because I was really at my apex then.

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:23.772
And I just remember my Mom coming in

00:37:23.772 --> 00:37:26.110
and I came in and starting
looking for excuses

00:37:26.110 --> 00:37:27.851
like I should've batted fourth today,

00:37:27.851 --> 00:37:32.070
it was sunny, she was like
"You struck out four times".

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:34.590
Meanwhile, this is the woman
who, when I opened the door

00:37:34.590 --> 00:37:38.200
for an elderly woman at
McDonald's once, treated it

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:41.850
as a Nobel Prize winning award behavior.

00:37:41.850 --> 00:37:46.850
So she created enormous self-esteem
but not delusion, right?

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:49.542
Listen, if you're five foot four

00:37:49.542 --> 00:37:51.517
and your parents are telling you,

00:37:51.517 --> 00:37:55.519
"Yes, you can go to the
NBA, Abraham" that's fine

00:37:55.519 --> 00:37:58.320
(audience laughs)

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:01.217
but it needs to be added
with "and you will be one

00:38:01.217 --> 00:38:04.333
"of the only three people
in the history of the sport"

00:38:05.330 --> 00:38:08.160
and oh by the way, Muggsy
Bogues and Spud Webb

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:09.470
could do all these other things

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:11.561
that you are showing that you cannot.

00:38:11.561 --> 00:38:12.610
(audience laughs)

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:15.780
This, you can be anything delusion

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.450
is what's leading to a lot of resentment

